From 245abd17bdf8d7b14f24e20aaabcd095df58aa1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dotheroes <dotheroes@localhost>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:08:41 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 40/40] Resident geometry: static draws stay in GPU memory
 (vm_watch)

The FIFO walker re-read, re-hashed and re-copied every draw's vertex
data each frame although the vertex cache saw ~100% hits and no change
(3D Dot Game Heroes: ~500 draws, 58 MB, ~9 ms of a ~21 ms frame).

- rsx_draw_engine: a draw's converted vertices and rebuilt 32-bit index
  list are kept by the backend (geom_create) under a key of everything
  they were built from besides memory: primitive, layout, vertex arrays
  and where they resolve, index array, the draw's ranges. The draw is
  drawn from there (draw_geom) while no guest page it was read from has
  been written; each range is watched before it is read. A written range
  rebuilds the entry at its next draw; one rebuilt again and again, or
  whose memory cannot be watched, stays on the copy path (dynamic).
  RSX_GEOM_CACHE=0 is the old path, RSX_GEOM_CACHE_STATS=1 reports,
  RSX_GEOM_CACHE_CHECK=1 rebuilds every hit too and compares.
- rsx_vulkan_backend: geom_* -- a linear arena of 64 MB chunks in
  device-local host-visible memory (host memory when there is none left),
  written once; draws bind it instead of copying into the batch ring.
- runtime/memory/vm_watch: which guest pages were written since a point
  in time, at no cost while nothing is. A watched page is read-only; the
  first write from any thread faults into a vectored handler that gives
  it a new epoch and makes it writable. The epoch is read before any
  protection, and protection changes are serialised, so no write between
  a watch and the read it guards is lost. Pages written over and over
  are left alone. Windows only for now (vm_watch_available() is 0
  elsewhere, and the engine keeps the copy path there).
- sys_fs, cellFs, cellHttp: a kernel write into guest memory (fread
  straight into the buffer, recv) would fail on a read-only page, so they
  release their range first (ppu_fs's fs_prefault already writes each
  page from user mode).

Measured on the walk route: the walker's drain 15.2 -> 4.8 ms a frame;
with a steady vblank (the port's own change) gameplay holds 60 fps. The
check mode found no missed write; the castle frame is pixel-identical
with the cache on and off.
